<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Steyr</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Steyr is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> and the Steyr rivers. Its castle was the seat of the Otakar counts, who gave their name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. For centuries the town prospered from the iron trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzberg">Erzberg</a>, and in modern times it became known for the Steyr arms and vehicle works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steyr.jpg" alt="Steyr" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Waghubinger (Lewenstein) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_ueber_die_Steyrer_Altstadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> settled the area from about 600 BCE, and their kingdom of Noricum became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in 15 BCE. The Roman Iron Road led from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzberg">Erzberg</a> mine along the Enns River to the camp of Lauriacum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Slavic settlers who moved in during the 6th century were defeated by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III of Bavaria</a>, who granted the land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kremsmunster-abbey">Kremsmünster Abbey</a> in 777.</p><p>A fortress above the Steyr River, built by the Traungau counts during the Hungarian invasions, is first mentioned as Styraburg in a deed of 980. From 1055 the castle and the adjacent March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> were ruled by the Otakar dynasty, who controlled the iron mining at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzberg">Erzberg</a> and made Steyr a centre of courtly culture and Middle High German poetry.</p><p>In 1180 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> raised Margrave Ottokar IV to Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, but the line died out in 1192 and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgenberg-pact">Georgenberg Pact</a> of 1186 his lands passed to the Babenberg dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Steyr then lost its importance as a residence but remained a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a>, producing mainly knives and arms.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> king Rudolf I won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle on the Marchfeld</a> in 1278, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium">King Albert I</a> confirmed the town privileges and market rights in 1287, and the citizens profited from a preferred place in the iron trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, above all with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In the 13th and 14th centuries Steyr was a centre of the Waldensian movement, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> spread quickly there from about 1525.</p><p>The iron trade declined during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and the Peasants' War of 1626. A fire in 1727 destroyed the medieval Styraburg, which the Baroque Lamberg Castle replaced. The Armistice of Steyr was signed there on 25 December 1800.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Steyr is a statutory city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, where the Steyr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> rivers meet beneath Lamberg Castle. The firm of Leopold Werndl, founded in 1830, became a stock company in 1864 and later the Steyr-Werke, which merged with Austro-Daimler and Puch in 1934. The town was heavily bombed in February 1944. Its historic centre around the Stadtplatz was restored after the war, and the Gothic Bummerlhaus is among its best-known buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Steyr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The March of Styria grew from a Carolingian border zone ruled from the 11th century by the Otakar counts of <b>Steyr</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-styria">Duchy of Styria</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1056 it was ruled by the Otakar family of <b>Steyr</b>, and the territory took its name, Styria (Steiermark), from their castle of Steyr in Upper Austria.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-styria">Margraviate of Styria</a></li></ul></section>
